const scopeExamples = "# Scope Examples: Before → After Rewrites\r\nHow to take a big idea and trim it to something finishable in 1–2 weeks, without losing what makes it interesting.\r\n \r\nUse these during the Narrow step when a student's idea is exciting but too large to execute.\r\n \r\n---\r\n \r\n## How to Use This File\r\n \r\nWhen a student describes a project, ask yourself: **Is this a 1-month idea being attempted in 1 week?**\r\n \r\nIf yes, use the pattern below:\r\n1. Name what's exciting about their big version (validate it)\r\n2. Identify the one core mechanic or value\r\n3. Cut everything that requires the full version to be useful\r\n4. Define a \"proof of concept\" version that delivers that core value alone\r\n---\r\n \r\n## Scope Rewrites by Category\r\n \r\n---\r\n \r\n### Games\r\n \r\n**Before:** \"A multiplayer battle royale game where 100 players fight on a shrinking map with different weapons and abilities.\"\r\n**After:** \"A 2-player local turn-based battle game with 3 weapons and a 10x10 grid map — playable in the browser.\"\r\n- What we kept: the combat core\r\n- What we cut: multiplayer infrastructure, map shrinking, ability system, matchmaking\r\n- Why it works: two people can actually play and enjoy it this week\r\n**Before:** \"An open-world RPG with quests, a crafting system, and a day/night cycle.\"\r\n**After:** \"A text adventure with 3 rooms, 2 items to find, and one puzzle to solve — with a hand-drawn map as a bonus visual.\"\r\n- What we kept: exploration and discovery\r\n- What we cut: graphics engine, quest trees, crafting, time systems\r\n- Why it works: the fun of RPGs is problem-solving and story, not the engine\r\n**Before:** \"A rhythm game like Guitar Hero with custom songs.\"\r\n**After:** \"A rhythm game with one song and 3 difficulty levels — hit the key on the beat, get a score.\"\r\n- What we kept: the core mechanic (timing + feedback)\r\n- What we cut: song library, custom uploads, multiplayer, leaderboard\r\n- Why it works: one song well-executed teaches everything\r\n---\r\n \r\n### Apps and Tools\r\n \r\n**Before:** \"A social media app where people share their art and follow each other.\"\r\n**After:** \"A personal gallery webpage — upload your own art, add a title and date, see it displayed in a clean grid.\"\r\n- What we kept: showing work beautifully\r\n- What we cut: accounts, followers, feeds, comments, moderation\r\n- Why it works: a beautiful personal gallery is valuable and shippable; a social network is a company\r\n**Before:** \"An app that tracks your fitness, logs meals, shows calories, and gives workout recommendations.\"\r\n**After:** \"A single-purpose workout log: enter today's exercise and duration, see a 7-day history chart.\"\r\n- What we kept: the habit and accountability loop\r\n- What we cut: meal tracking, calorie database, AI recommendations, integrations\r\n- Why it works: the log itself creates the behavior change — the rest is polish\r\n**Before:** \"A marketplace where people can buy and sell handmade goods.\"\r\n**After:** \"A single seller storefront page — 6 product cards with photos, prices, and a 'Contact to order' link.\"\r\n- What we kept: showcasing products and inviting buyers\r\n- What we cut: payments, listings management, buyer accounts, search, reviews\r\n- Why it works: a DM-to-order model works at small scale and has zero payment infrastructure risk\r\n**Before:** \"A tutoring platform where teachers can post lessons and students can submit assignments.\"\r\n**After:** \"A single-subject study guide webpage — structured notes, 5 practice problems, and a self-check answer key.\"\r\n- What we kept: the learning value\r\n- What we cut: accounts, submission system, teacher dashboard, notifications\r\n- Why it works: the content is the product; the platform is a v3 problem\r\n---\r\n \r\n### Data and Research\r\n \r\n**Before:** \"An analysis of every country's economic data since 1900 to find patterns.\"\r\n**After:** \"A data story comparing 5 countries' GDP growth over the last 30 years, with 3 charts and a 300-word interpretation.\"\r\n- What we kept: the analytical insight and storytelling\r\n- What we cut: 120 years of data, 195 countries, complex modeling\r\n- Why it works: a focused comparison makes a stronger argument than a sprawling one\r\n**Before:** \"A live dashboard showing real-time stock prices, news sentiment, and trading signals.\"\r\n**After:** \"A static analysis of one company's stock price over the past year, with annotations for key events.\"\r\n- What we kept: the financial insight\r\n- What we cut: real-time data feeds, sentiment analysis APIs, trading logic\r\n- Why it works: historical analysis teaches the same skills without the infrastructure\r\n**Before:** \"A survey of everyone at my school about their mental health, with statistics and recommendations.\"\r\n**After:** \"A short illustrated explainer — 'What stress actually does to your brain' — based on 3 published studies, shared as a PDF or web page.\"\r\n- What we kept: the educational impact on the audience\r\n- What we cut: data collection from real students, privacy considerations, statistical analysis\r\n- Why it works: you don't need original data to make something meaningful and shareable\r\n---\r\n \r\n### Creative and Design\r\n \r\n**Before:** \"An animated short film with original characters, voice acting, and a full story arc.\"\r\n**After:** \"A 60-second animated loop — one character, one environment, one repeated action — with original music or sound.\"\r\n- What we kept: the animation craft and atmosphere\r\n- What we cut: story arc, multiple characters, voice acting, editing\r\n- Why it works: a polished loop teaches the same skills as a 10-minute film\r\n**Before:** \"A magazine with articles, photo essays, interviews, and a design system.\"\r\n**After:** \"A 4-page zine: one essay (400 words), two images, one pull quote, and a cover — exported as a PDF.\"\r\n- What we kept: editorial voice and visual design\r\n- What we cut: volume of content, ongoing publishing schedule, team\r\n- Why it works: one great issue > ten mediocre ones\r\n**Before:** \"A font I designed from scratch with a full character set and multiple weights.\"\r\n**After:** \"A display font with just uppercase letters (A–Z), displayed on a specimen page with 3 example uses.\"\r\n- What we kept: the design process and the artifact\r\n- What we cut: lowercase, numbers, punctuation, italics, weights, OpenType features\r\n- Why it works: 26 characters done well is a real deliverable; 200+ is a career\r\n---\r\n \r\n### Community and Advocacy\r\n \r\n**Before:** \"An app that connects volunteers with local nonprofits and tracks hours.\"\r\n**After:** \"A single-page directory of 10–15 local volunteer opportunities with descriptions, links, and contact info — updated manually.\"\r\n- What we kept: connecting people to opportunities\r\n- What we cut: accounts, matching algorithm, hour tracking, nonprofit onboarding\r\n- Why it works: a curated list people can actually find and use is more valuable than a half-built platform\r\n**Before:** \"A petition site where anyone can create and sign petitions on any topic.\"\r\n**After:** \"One petition page for one specific issue — with a clear ask, a short explanation, and a Google Form to collect signatures.\"\r\n- What we kept: mobilizing people around an issue\r\n- What we cut: user-generated petitions, authentication, spam prevention, moderation\r\n- Why it works: the issue you care about is the project; the platform is a distraction\r\n---\r\n \r\n## The Scope Rewrite Formula\r\n \r\nWhen helping a student scope down, use this structure:\r\n \r\n> \"What I love about your idea is [the core value]. The version that takes a month to build adds [feature A, B, C] on top of that core. But you can get all the way to [core value] by just building [minimal version]. Here's what that looks like: [one-sentence description]. What do you think — does that still feel exciting?\"\r\n \r\n---\r\n \r\n## Red Flags That Signal a Scope Problem\r\n \r\n- The idea requires other people to sign up or create accounts to be useful\r\n- The idea requires real-time data from an external service\r\n- The idea has more than one \"main\" user (e.g., both students AND teachers)\r\n- The student describes it as \"like [major app] but...\"\r\n- The student hasn't mentioned what version 1 looks like — only the final vision\r\nWhen you spot these, ask: **\"What's the smallest version of this that you'd actually be proud to show someone?\"**";
export default scopeExamples;
